   "l": "The five colours make a person's eyes go blind;\nthe five tones make a person's ears go deaf;\nthe five flavours make a person's mouth go wrong;\nhard riding and the chase make a person's heart break out in madness;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The five colours leave the eye sightless; the five tones leave the ear deaf; the five flavours blunt the palate; hard riding and the chase set the heart raving;",
   "n": "令 sets up a pivot: 令 | 人 | 目盲, 'cause | a person | eye-blind'. One verb governs a whole small clause, and no 'to be' is needed inside it. The fives are the standard sets of the age — five colours (blue-green, yellow, red, white, black), the five notes of the pentatonic scale, five tastes (sour, bitter, sweet, pungent, salt); the complaint is not against colour but against the graded catalogue of refinement, which wears the senses out. 爽 is the character to watch: in modern Mandarin it means 'refreshing, delightful', and classically it means to err, to go astray — so of a palate, to lose its discrimination. The classical sense is the one to hold, here and everywhere.",
   "p": true
