   "l": "Therefore the sage works for the belly and not for the eye;\nand so he puts that away and takes this.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "So the sage provides for the belly and not for the eye: he lets the one go and holds to the other.",
   "n": "為 is a real choice here, and the choice changes the pronunciation. Read wèi it is 'for the sake of' — the sage works for the belly; read wéi it is 'attends to, makes his business'. Wang Bi's comment points the same way on either road: the man who serves the belly uses things to feed himself, the man who serves the eye lets things make a servant of him. We read wèi, and the alternative is a genuine one. 彼 'that' and 此 'this' are the far and the near: the eye reaches outside for what it has not got, the belly is filled at home."
  }
